================================================================================
A-1 (advisory, class, pre-existing) — reds whose command lives outside the repo
================================================================================
red 02's header command is "sh /tmp/claude-1000/.../scratchpad/w042-mutants.sh".
The script itself says "Not committed". It is session-scoped and will not survive
the session, so the header cannot be rerun from the repo. The standing rule is
"Mechanical work is a script you write, run and keep."
This is a class. Search: grep '^# command:' studio/ci/reds/**, filtered for
/tmp|scratchpad. There are 12 instances:
  W-020/09, 10, 11, 13, 14, 15, 16;  W-028/18, 19, 21;  W-036/02;  W-042/02.
W-042 contributes one instance and did not start the pattern. Recommend a lesson
and, per standing rules, a check rule rather than prose: advise when a red's
"# command:" references a path outside the repo. Not blocking W-042.

================================================================================
A-2 (advisory, single instance) — the harness exit is OR, not AND
================================================================================
w042-mutants.sh sets overall=1 if EITHER mutant exits nonzero. So "# exit: 1"
would still be recorded if only one mutant died. This log is not affected: the
summary line "mutant1 ... exit=1  mutant2 ... exit=1" and both FAIL blocks show
that both died. Any future multi-mutant harness should require every mutant to
die before it exits 1.

================================================================================
A-3 (advisory, single instance) — commit message overclaims restoration
================================================================================
ca002a5 says "git diff on the mutation shows empty before/after each". The log
prints git diff once, after both mutants. The between-mutant revert is proven by
behaviour (see red 02, point 1), not by a printed diff. The evidence is sound;
the prose describing it is inaccurate.

